  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Organization of Trade Unions (NOTU) developed a position paper on climate change which was validated by its leaders, then presented for negotiation and adopted 19th August 2022 by the Tripartite Labour Advisory Board. The policy advocates healthy, ecological, decent and job-rich green growth based on just transition principles. It highlights the need for effective engagement between government and social partners as well as other civil society actors on climate change challenges and issues of green and resilient growth opportunities in all appropriate forums to inform the design, formulation, adoption, implementation, monitoring, evaluation and regular review of policies and programs relating to climate change.    

In addition, NOTU signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) 21st February 2022 with Private Employment Agencies, which, among other things, called for the establishment of a &quot;call center&quot;, where migrant workers can file complaints regarding violations and abuse of their rights and inquire about any labour migration. Also, NOTU established a Migrant Resource Centre which offers a wide range of information about migrant workers&#x2019; rights, mechanisms for dispute settlement, job opportunities, etc. The ILO provided technical assistance that enabled NOTU to critically analyse Uganda&#x2019;s policy and strategies on the shift towards a greener economy, Nationally Determined Contributions, and the UN Sustainable Development Goal 13, as well as the ILO Just Transition Guidelines and Resolutions on the Green Economy. This technical assistance then informed the development of NOTU&apos;s position paper on green economy and resilient recovery based on job-rich growth and just transition.

The ILO organized a national workshop for NOTU and its affiliated sectoral trade unions that informed the validation of their position paper on climate change and just transition aimed at tackling climate-induced disasters and preservation of the environment to achieve the creation decent, green job-rich growth. 

Within the framework of the development cooperation project funded by the United Kingdom for Better Regional Migration Management (BRMM), the ILO co-facilitated several workshops at the national and regional levels in which the NOTU participated.</narrative>
   </description>
